  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, a ridge remains centered over the Southeast. At \nthe surface, a stalling boundary is stretched over Kentucky and west \nTennessee. Currently, showers and storms are present upstream along \nthe aforementioned boundary. CAMs have this line dissipating before \nor as it moves into our region but a few stray showers are possible \nmainly near the Upper Cumberland Plateau and Southwest Virginia. \nToday, the front will lift north but a few showers will be possible \nmainly in Southwest Virginia. Today will be hot again with highs in \nthe 80s. All three climate sites are forecast to break or tie the \nrecord high today. CHA and TRI broke their record highs yesterday \n(Wed). \n\nRecord highs Today: \nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville     Tri-Cities      Oak Ridge \n                 \n10-15   85(1950)       87(1897)       83(1971)       83(2021)  \n\nThursday night, a deep trough will move into the Midwest and into \nthe Ohio Valley by afternoon. The surface low will move through the \nGreat Lakes and into New England bringing a cold front through the \nTennessee Valley in the late afternoon or early evening hours on \nFriday. Winds will be gusty Friday afternoon and evening with gusts \nup to 30 mph likely at times. The best chance for showers will be \nSouthwest Virginia, Northeast Tennessee and along the East Tennessee \nmountains and foothills in the late afternoon and evening hours. \nThunder chances will be low with low instability expected.\n\nThe weekend will be dry and much cooler with highs in the upper 50s \nand lower 60s Saturday. Only slightly warmer on Sunday with highs in \nthe mid 60s. Temperatures will be cold enough for frost on Saturday \nand Sunday nights mainly in Southwest Virginia, Northeast Tennessee \nand the higher elevations. \n\nEarly next week will be dry with highs warming into the 70s by \nTuesday as ridging and high pressure dominate the Southeast.",
